      <h1>Bruno vs Postman: Which API Client Should Your Team Use?</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">Files in your repo vs a cloud platform: what actually changes for your team</p>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-lg border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50/60 p-6 dark:border-indigo-500/30 dark:bg-indigo-500/5">
        <h2 class="mt-0 text-lg font-semibold text-indigo-900 dark:text-indigo-200">TL;DR</h2>
        <p class="mb-0 text-indigo-900/90 dark:text-indigo-200/90">Bruno vs Postman boils down to how your team works: Bruno stores collections as files in your repo with no account required, while Postman synchronizes collections through Postman Cloud.</p>
        <ul class="mb-0 mt-4 space-y-2 text-indigo-900/90 dark:text-indigo-200/90">
          <li><strong>Pick <a href="/tools/bruno/" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400">Bruno</a> if</strong> your team already reviews everything in pull requests, you want collections stored as plain files with no vendor account involved, and you mainly need an API client plus a CI runner.</li>
          <li><strong>Pick <a href="/tools/postman/" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400">Postman</a> if</strong> you need hosted mock servers, scheduled monitors, published documentation, or collaborators who don't use Git.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400"><strong>Independence note.</strong> devxtools.dev has no relationship with Bruno or Postman. Both vendors publish their own comparison pages (usebruno.com/compare, postman.com/alternatives). We treat those as vendor claims. Prices were checked on the official pricing pages on <strong>2026-10-15</strong>. The CI commands below were run on 2026-10-15 against a local test API with <strong>Bru CLI 4.2.1</strong> and <strong>Newman 6.2.3</strong> on Node 20.19 (Linux). Both passed and wrote JUnit XML.</p>

      <h2 id="at-a-glance">Bruno vs Postman at a glance</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th></th>
              <th>Bruno</th>
              <th>Postman</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
